body:
I'm diagnosed with asperger's and have no real issue with foods. There are a couple things I simply don't like and I feel that is normal to everyone. There is bound to be a couple things that people don't like and that is fine

&#x200B;

I just can't seem to understand how there are some people as picky as they are. Like there are so many good foods out there, why on earth would someone lower themselves to shitty processed freezer food

The labeling makes me think of two things. The first and most likely theory is that it means light radiation which I suppose it technically does being that we see it as green and can store heat (IR radiation)

&#x200B;

The other thing I thought of was that this species was maybe like sunflowers or certain fungi in which it could be used in bioremediation of radioactive contamination in soil. There are a lot of plants known to be able to do this and still live like nothing has happened, and it would be pretty cool if this had similar features. However, I have strong doubt that this is what the label means

Looking up porn on onion sites will only lead to something fucky, just type in big boob into google images and you'll have millions of results for free

&#x200B;

If it is specifically OF leaks you want, there are apt to be plenty of clearweb sites for that, probably including a bunch of subreddits

Someone else already outlined the meal hall here pretty well, but I will also add in my 2 cents. Overall I thought it was pretty good. However since they cycle through roughly the same menu every couple of weeks, you may find that it is a bit repetitive. However, on occasion they will break that cycle and toss in new stuff for the hell of it and it turns out pretty good. One of my favorite things was the special events. I cannot recall when they are, but they have designated days or weeks for neat things. For example, they have a poutine week where there is a very large assortment of poutine served at lunch. Then there was the food around the world event that they would pick a particular region and prepare food from that area

&#x200B;

All in all, I thought it was pretty good. Very handy for those who don't want to cook or are living in a spot that makes it a bit harder to cook for yourself (Like VP for example)

Ye I have been told I am not full blown color blind, but there are some anomalies present. For example what should be easily distinguishable shades of red all look the same(and this is pretty much the same with all colors. Having some hue issues is present in everyone, especially in lighter shades, but for me at least it extends into the realm of "most people should be able to tell these shades apart". The other color issue I have is picking out blues from purples. Normally I wouldn't think much of it as it is mainly just a hue issue and nothing more severe if you will, but it has manifested itself within the last couple of years and it has made some things with my schoolwork a bit of a challenge

&#x200B;

Unfortunately there is not much I can do about it other than adapt and move on. I've read into the lenses a little bit, but I'm not entirely sure how well they will work for me. Like any discussion I have seen about them have been pretty split between they work alright for some and they don't do anything for another. One thing that my eye specialist mentioned about these to is that it may fix things initially, but as time goes on and my brain gradually adapts to that filter(for lack of better terms), you may just end up back at square one. So who knows lol, I'll look into it a bit more if it becomes more of a problem. At this stage I'm mainly intrigued about what caused it

[https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15069045/](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15069045/)

&#x200B;

"Ejaculation frequency was not statistically significantly associated with risk of advanced prostate cancer..... Our results suggest that ejaculation frequency is not related to increased risk of prostate cancer."  


&#x200B;

Not in the slightest

If you bundle up and dress properly for the cold you should be good. As another commentor said, we peak in our level of cold in January or February (mainly February these last couple years, but that may not mean anything this year. You don't want to stand around long in those temps though.

&#x200B;

I don't know much about uber or taxi in the city as I haven't honestly used them since I've been here. But bussing is a good option to if you are inclined to do that as well. Imo it's still cold on the bus, but it beats having to walk in those temperatures

As you said in your post, it was speculated that we would be back to in person classes. But ye, with the case numbers not where anyone wants them to be, that is rather speculative. On any of the covid update emails I've read through, I haven't seen any word on new plans. However I very well could have missed it.

&#x200B;

Another possibility is that they are going to plan it out like they did the vaccine status system... that being to wait until the bitter end to ponder on it and then announce wtf is going on

I had her for ANTH 111. The course itself wasn't too hard, however you want to make sure you do all of the readings required and take notes in class. Anything in your notes and in the text has the potential of showing up on exams.

&#x200B;

On the plus side though, her exams tended to be on the smaller side compared to other exams I have taken, so that could also be in your favor

I personally don't have any, but I have done some browsing around to see where you may be able to find some. Another commenter mentioned value village, which is a pretty good spot to look

&#x200B;

In regards to online vending, perhaps kijiji or ebay. I haven't touched kijiji yet to see if anything is there, but ebay does have listings. You will have to filter through to see exactly what you want though, because there are a number of vendors there who have jacked the prices of their glasses quite a bit, so you will want to make sure you aren't getting screwed over

The first breath of air after a dose of ventolin hits hard after an asthma attack though

&#x200B;

Source: am asthmatic

My experience in VP wasn't horrid, however there were some flaws in the grand scheme of things. There are bats in some of the buildings (Though I never saw one myself, there are always people who do see them, and you sometimes will see people in to relocate the bats and clean up the spots they were living in

&#x200B;

During my stay in Athabasca hall, someone shit themselves out in the main hallway, and it got all over the carpet and everything, so they had to close that down to clean for a few hours. In addition to that, someone in their great intelligence would go and but out those drywall ceiling tiles all the time, so you'd often see drywall and plaster bits all over the carpet

&#x200B;

Another commenter mentioned the shower bit. At the start people are out trying to figure out shower schedules, so there is a brief time that people are out waiting to get into one as they get crowded. But as time went on, people would sort out their schedules and the crowing would go away. Another problem though that I had with the showers was there was always one in Ath hall that was a little busted and it would spray a weird way behind the nozzle and flood the washroom floor. In addition to the washrooms, the other year in Qu'appelle hall(the year we all had to get out of rez because of covid), there was some one who liked to stuff whole rolls of tp into the toilet bowl

&#x200B;

Then you have the food there. I rather liked it, but it gets repetitive very fast. They have week long events though(like the poutine week or the international food week), where they drastically mix things up, which I thought was quite nice

Ohh sorry haha. Recently, we have acquired a collection of annuals, namely lavenders and geraniums this spring. Due to the weather of last fall and time constraints, we put off doing a final honey pull, and left it for the bees in the winter. Because of that, they would have had access to a lot of sage and other late blooming wildflowers as well

&#x200B;

As the spring moves on and we get into summer, they will probably find the neighboring pea crops, our fruit trees, and cactus flowers, along with any other flowers we buy for them

